  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\n19Z water vapor imagery showed a shortwave lifting through the \nmiddle MO river valley while the longwave upper trough remained over \nthe Pacific Northwest. Surface obs showed an inverted surface trough \nbisecting the forecast area with CNK showing a northeast wind and FOE\nwith a southeast wind. Cloud cover and limited mixing of the \nboundary layer has kept temps in the 80s and lower 90s so far today. \n\nModels show the airmass over the forecast area remains conditionally \nunstable with MU CAPE values 3000 J/kg. But think some subsidence on \nthe back side of the shortwave over eastern NEB has limited vertical \nmotion so far this afternoon with some modest inhibition for a \nsurface parcel. Think there are a couple mesoscale features to watch \nfor that could spark convection. The first would be continued \ndestabilization along the surface boundary and weak convergence. As \nthe shortwave continues to move east subsidence will weaken. Mid \nlevel lapse rates remain steep at around 8 C/km and forecast \nsounding prog around 1200 J/kg of downdraft CAPE. Meanwhile overall \nbulk shear is progged to weaken. So for the late afternoon and early \nevening there could be a pulse storm or two develop mainly along the \nsurface boundary. Damaging winds could occur with a collapsing \nthunderstorms, and with precipital water values near 2 inches \ntorrential rainfall will remain possible. The second mesoscale \nfeature to keep an eye on is the potential for a convectively \ninduced vort max lifting out of the high plains late tonight. \nAlready seeing convection developing over CO and models show a \ncompact vort max lifting through north central KS towards sunrise \nSaturday. There should be quite a bit more inhibition with any \nshower activity being well elevated. Bulk shear is expected to be \nweek overnight, so again pulse storms could bring some brief heavy \nrainfall. Lows tonight are forecast to fall into the middle and \nupper 70s for most locations. \n\nFor Saturday models show the upper ridge sliding south with the \nsurface boundary remaining in the area. Have held onto some slight \nchance POPs across north central KS through the morning thinking \nthat elevated shower activity may be able to linger similar to the \npast couple days. Confidence in the temp forecast is below normal as \nsome of the short term guidance is coming in with a more southerly \nposition to the surface boundary. This brings into question the heat \nwarning. Think that some of the northern counties may struggle to \nreach the 105 heat index. But if it clears out and we get a \nsoutherly wind we may still see heat indices of 105 to 110 for areas \nalong and south of the KS river. So have opted to leave the \nexcessive heat warning as is. Models show improving storm chances \nSaturday night, and given the pattern over the last several days it \nis hard to argue with. The central plains should be under more of \nthe westerlies and the persistent nocturnal convection seen to the \nnorth of the area the last couple nights will have a better \nopportunity to develop over northern KS. So have stuck with the \nlikely POPs across the northern counties that the NBM has. \n\nThere is pretty good agreement from the operational models and \nensembles for there to be a break from the excessive heat Sunday and \nMonday. The good news is highs look to be in the 80s. The bad news \nis the humidity doesn't look that much better with dewpoints \nexpected to still be around 70 degrees on Monday. Shortwave energy \nwithin northwest flow aloft is progged to bring chances for shower \nand storms to the area Wednesday. By the end of the forecast period, \nthe consensus from the ensembles is for the upper ridge to redevelop \nover the Rockies. There is a lot more spread in the ensembles \n(around 15 degrees between the twenty fifth and seventy fifth \npercentiles) overall for Thursday and Friday, so haven't made any \nchanges to the forecast from WPC/NBM. I would anticipate adjustments \nto the forecast temps for the back half of next week. Overall the \nsignal from the ensembles is for scattered precip chances that lines \nup with climatology.",
